Bill.Woodward@bbs.acs.unc.edu (Bill Woodward) writes:

> There has been a bit of talk here about identifying gems.
> I don't know if this has already been discussed, but the
> way I handle it, is that every time I find a store that
> will buy gems, I drop all that I am carrying.  The shop-
> keeper will buy them from you if they are worth anything,
> and will not pay you for them if they are worthless.  Now,
> pick them back up, and #name them.  When asked if you want
> to name an individual item, answer no.  Name the gems that
> are worth something "worthy (color) gem", and the ones that
> are worthless "worthless (color) gem".  This seems very
> simple, but the advantage is that you are naming classes
> of gems.  So, if you were carrying 2 blue gems, each in a
> different inventory slot, went into a store, sold them and
> named them, from now on all the sapphires (or whatever) will
> be called "worthy blue gems" and all the pieces of blue
> glass will be called "worthless blue gems" and can be ignored.
>    Incidentally, this method also works for sacks and lamps,
> so if you find one lamp in a tool store that is ordinary, you
> don't have to buy all the lamps there to find out which ones
> are magical.  Buy the first one, and apply it.  If the room
> lights up, then it is ordinary.  Name it and all the ordinary
> lamps will now be named ordinary lamps.  All the other lamps
> will be magical.  I used this method one time in a huge tool
> store nad wound up with three magical lamps, only having to
> buy one ordinary one (the first).  Saves getting charged for
> charges.
> 				Bill Woodward

